1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:01,961
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,961 --> 00:00:05,476
Let's discuss what the project
closure process means for you,

3
00:00:05,476 --> 00:00:07,550
the project manager.

4
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:11,365
For a project manager, it's important
to close the project properly for

5
00:00:11,365 --> 00:00:12,480
a number of reasons.

6
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,005
Closing provides an opportunity
to reflect on how you and

7
00:00:16,005 --> 00:00:19,384
your team performed, ensure
every task is completed, and

8
00:00:19,384 --> 00:00:22,698
prevent confusion around
the project in the future.

9
00:00:22,698 --> 00:00:26,840
I'll detail the importance of
these closing practices, as well as

10
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:30,917
the necessary documentation to
successfully close a project.

11
00:00:30,917 --> 00:00:34,313
One of the most important aspects
of thoroughly closing a project is

12
00:00:34,313 --> 00:00:36,670
the project closeout report.

13
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,530
A project closeout report is a document
created by project managers for

14
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:46,799
project managers. A project closeout
report serves three major purposes.

15
00:00:46,799 --> 00:00:50,736
First, it's a blueprint to
document what the team did,

16
00:00:50,736 --> 00:00:53,533
how they did it, and what they delivered.

17
00:00:53,533 --> 00:00:58,143
Second, it provides an evaluation
of the quality of work. And third,

18
00:00:58,143 --> 00:01:03,481
it evaluates the project's performance
with respect to budget and schedule.

19
00:01:03,481 --> 00:01:08,380
Similar to the retrospective, the project
closeout report can be used to determine

20
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:11,283
best practices when it
comes to future projects.

21
00:01:11,283 --> 00:01:15,846
Think of it as a transfer of knowledge
from you to future project managers.

22
00:01:15,846 --> 00:01:20,245
For instance, once a project closes,
there's a chance a similar project or

23
00:01:20,245 --> 00:01:24,137
a continuation of that project will
come up within the organization,

24
00:01:24,137 --> 00:01:27,015
and maybe a different
project manager is assigned

25
00:01:27,015 --> 00:01:29,970
to this new project while
you go on to other things.

26
00:01:29,970 --> 00:01:34,192
If you create an in-depth closeout report,
it will help the newly-assigned

27
00:01:34,192 --> 00:01:38,973
project manager immensely to know what
happened previously on a similar project.

28
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:42,395
Your closeout report could include
things that worked out well and

29
00:01:42,395 --> 00:01:44,360
things that didn't work out so well.

30
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:48,447
Creating a closeout report will also
reduce the time you spend answering

31
00:01:48,447 --> 00:01:51,540
questions as the new project
manager gets up to speed.

32
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:55,719
It's safe to assume that the people
reading your report in the future will be

33
00:01:55,719 --> 00:01:59,131
unfamiliar with the project,
so be as detailed as possible.

34
00:01:59,131 --> 00:02:03,629
That way the new project team will be able
to form an understanding of the purpose,

35
00:02:03,629 --> 00:02:07,230
execution, and outcome of the project
based on your report alone.

36
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:12,502
In your project closeout report, you'll
want to include the following things:

37
00:02:12,502 --> 00:02:14,041
An executive summary:

38
00:02:14,041 --> 00:02:18,182
this means a description of the process
and what the purpose of the project was.

39
00:02:18,182 --> 00:02:23,561
This should be short and concise;
a few sentences to a paragraph, maximum.

40
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:27,031
If an executive did not have time
to read the entire document and

41
00:02:27,031 --> 00:02:31,353
only had time to read this executive
summary, would they be able to understand

42
00:02:31,353 --> 00:02:35,673
the project highlights? A list of key
accomplishments: think of this as a way to

43
00:02:35,673 --> 00:02:41,310
highlight the team's accomplishments, as
well as the overall impact of the project.

44
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:45,252
Include any lessons learned,
like: what went well, and why?

45
00:02:45,252 --> 00:02:47,210
What went wrong, and why?

46
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:51,930
What were the major effects of key
problem areas, such as scope creep and

47
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:53,052
schedule slip?

48
00:02:53,052 --> 00:02:57,707
Any open items: this could be things
you didn't quite get to or ideas for

49
00:02:57,707 --> 00:03:00,851
changes you'd make if
you'd had the time.

50
00:03:00,851 --> 00:03:04,966
Your next steps: things like, are there
expected follow up projects, and

51
00:03:04,966 --> 00:03:07,720
is there any ongoing maintenance required?

52
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:12,913
You also want to include important
information about the schedule and

53
00:03:12,913 --> 00:03:17,333
important deadlines, like: what were your
milestones, and how did you choose them?

54
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:19,430
How long did the project take?

55
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:25,001
Did the project stay on track? And
any information about major setbacks.

56
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:29,279
Be sure to list resources and
team members. Explain who is involved and

57
00:03:29,279 --> 00:03:30,739
what their roles were.

58
00:03:30,739 --> 00:03:35,036
This is also a key way to acknowledge
people who contributed to the project's

59
00:03:35,036 --> 00:03:39,213
completion. And finally, include
a resources and project archive section.

60
00:03:39,213 --> 00:03:43,002
This will include links to things
like your original project plan,

61
00:03:43,002 --> 00:03:47,533
any documented stakeholder communication
and feedback (like meeting notes),

62
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,017
and documentation used to track,
monitor, and report,

63
00:03:51,017 --> 00:03:55,946
and any technical information related
to the project deliverables like user

64
00:03:55,946 --> 00:03:57,322
guides and manuals.

65
00:03:57,322 --> 00:04:01,879
Hopefully by now, you understand how
project closeout reports are beneficial to

66
00:04:01,879 --> 00:04:06,150
not only the organization, but
the project manager as well.

67
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:10,240
Project closeout reports promote
visibility among team members and

68
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,714
create more efficient future projects.

69
00:04:12,714 --> 00:04:16,319
All right, we've covered a lot
of information. Great job for

70
00:04:16,319 --> 00:04:17,413
sticking with it!

71
00:04:17,413 --> 00:04:18,328
In the next video,

72
00:04:18,328 --> 00:04:21,660
we'll revisit the concepts you've
learned as we recap the module.
